{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794994451,"nonce":"UEr5LaDWlM+RbzblIfDMOiEfG0U=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgUriZw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/207","origin":"github"},"title":"rcwa: a permittivity edge on a grid sample is placed by rounding, giving a first-order error","message":"`jno.rcwa` samples the permittivity on its grid at points where a material edge commonly lies. Which side the edge sample falls on is then decided by floating-point rounding. For a lamellar grating with fill 0.5 written as `y \u003c 0.5 * period`, the edge sample lands inside the ridge: 129 of 256 samples, a fill of 0.504 instead of 0.5. The efficiencies converge to the wrong value, and the error falls only at first order in the grid size.\n\nThe grating tutorial (`docs/tutorial_examples/12_rcwa/01_binary_grating_orders.py`) writes its ridge the same way (`x \u003c DUTY * period`) and reports the resulting floor as truncation: \"converge the reflectance here to about 2e-3 of an exact-coefficient reference\".\n\nReproduced on release 0.4.2 and on `main` (b3dcdd22).\n\n## Reproduction\n\nLamellar grating, period 0.6, fill 0.5, `eps = 11`, thickness 0.2, wavelength 1, normal incidence, TE, 40 orders. The only difference between the two columns is a margin of 1e-9 on the edge.\n\n```python\nimport jax\njax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport numpy as np\nimport jno\n\nK0, HEIGHT, EPS, THICK, Z0 = 2 * np.pi, 1.0, 11.0, 0.2, 0.4\nPX, PY = 0.3, 0.6  # ridges along x, permittivity varies along y: TE\n\n\ndef reflectance(grid, margin, orders=40):\n    ridge = lambda x, y, z: jnp.where((Z0 \u003c z) \u0026 (z \u003c Z0 + THICK) \u0026 (y \u003c 0.5 * PY - margin), 1.0, 0.0)\n    d = jno.shape.box(0, 0, 0, PX, PY, HEIGHT, size=0.25).domain()\n    e = 1e-6\n    for tag, where in ((\"bottom\", lambda x, y, z: z \u003c e), (\"top\", lambda x, y, z: z \u003e HEIGHT - e), (\"left\", lambda x, y, z: x \u003c e),\n                       (\"right\", lambda x, y, z: x \u003e PX - e), (\"front\", lambda x, y, z: y \u003c e), (\"back\", lambda x, y, z: y \u003e PY - e)):\n        d.tag(tag, where)\n    u, v = d.fem_symbols()\n    xi, yi, zi, _ = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)\n    ui, vi = u.bind(x=xi, y=yi, z=zi), v.bind(x=xi, y=yi, z=zi)\n\n    def on(tag):\n        s = d.variable(tag, split=True)\n        return u.bind(x=s[0], y=s[1], z=s[2]), v.bind(x=s[0], y=s[1], z=s[2])\n\n    (ut, vt), (ub, vb) = on(\"top\"), on(\"bottom\")\n    eps = 1.0 + (EPS - 1.0) * jno.fn(ridge, [xi, yi, zi])\n    problem = jno.rcwa(\n        [ui.x * vi.x + ui.y * vi.y + ui.z * vi.z - K0**2 * eps * (u * vi), -(1j * K0 * ut) * vt, -(1j * K0 * ub - 2j * K0) * vb,\n         on(\"left\")[0] - on(\"right\")[0], on(\"front\")[0] - on(\"back\")[0]],\n        orders=orders, grid=grid,\n    )\n    return float(jnp.real(problem.solve().efficiency(\"R\")))\n\n\nfor grid in (256, 512, 1024):\n    r0, r1 = reflectance(grid, 0.0), reflectance(grid, 1e-9)\n    print(f\"grid {grid}: R = {r0:.6f} with y \u003c 0.5 * period, {r1:.6f} with a margin of 1e-9, difference {r0 - r1:+.2e}\")\n```\n\n| Grid | `y \u003c 0.5 * period` | `y \u003c 0.5 * period - 1e-9` | Difference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 256 | 0.002031 | 0.003401 | -1.37e-03 |\n| 512 | 0.002693 | 0.003391 | -6.98e-04 |\n| 1024 | 0.003038 | 0.003389 | -3.51e-04 |\n\nWith the margin the reflectance is stable to 1e-5 over the three grids. Without it the value is 40 % low at grid 256, and the difference halves each time the grid doubles. An independent implementation with exact Fourier coefficients agrees with the right-hand column (difference 5.6e-5 at 160 orders on grid 256).\n\nOn 0.4.2, `smoothing=k` behaves like a grid `k` times finer; it does not remove the first-order error.\n\n## Possible fixes\n\n- Sample the permittivity at cell centres, `(j + 0.5) * period / grid`, so that an edge at a simple fraction of the period never coincides with a sample.\n- Or average the permittivity over each grid cell (the Fourier coefficients of a piecewise-constant profile are then exact for edges on cell boundaries).\n- Correct the tutorial's statement: the 2e-3 it reports is this edge error, not the truncation of the method.","files":null}]}